<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>
    <data>
        <!--
            The Auditor. Specification §3's roles table names an `Auditor` /
            `audit_viewer` with "read-only access to audit logs"; INT-10 §4.3
            recorded that no such group existed in Cadra, and handoff 23's
            acceptance criterion — that the five §13.4 reports be openable by
            an auditor, PROVED BY ACTING — could not be met without one.

            It grants six READ scopes and no write scope of any kind. That is
            the whole design: in Cadra every API route runs `sudo()`, so an
            Odoo group is not the gate and cannot be; the SCOPE is the gate.
            A group that grants only read scopes therefore produces a session
            token that cannot write, which is a property of the token rather
            than of a permission row somebody can edit.

            Implied by nothing and implying nothing, deliberately. An auditor
            who is also an Asset Manager is not independent of what they are
            auditing. Do not hang this under an existing group to "tidy" the
            hierarchy — the same instruction the Tag-Issuing Unit carries,
            for a related reason.

            `myservice_api/tests/test_auditor_access.py` proves both halves by
            acting: every one of the five reports opens for a user holding
            exactly this group, and every write is refused.
        -->
        <!--
            Handoff 41: on Odoo 19 a group reaches the user form only through
            a `res.groups.privilege` (see the comment in
            `myservice_asset_register/security/asset_security.xml`). Without
            one, the Auditor was assignable only from the Groups menu in
            developer mode. Its own privilege, in the asset register's
            category, so it renders as a separate "No / Auditor" choice that
            can be set beside any register role — display grouping only, no
            implication either way.
        -->
        <record id="privilege_audit" model="res.groups.privilege">
            <field name="name">Audit</field>
            <field name="sequence">30</field>
            <field name="category_id" ref="myservice_asset_register.module_category_nrs_assets"/>
            <field name="description">Read-only access to the five standard audit reports. Independent of the register roles: an auditor who also manages assets is not independent of what they audit.</field>
        </record>

        <record id="group_auditor" model="res.groups">
            <field name="name">Auditor</field>
            <field name="privilege_id" ref="privilege_audit"/>
            <field name="comment">Read-only access for internal and external audit (specification §3, §13.4; INT-10). Grants the read scopes the standard audit reports and the register export need — the exact set is pinned in tests/test_auditor_access.py — and no write scope of any kind. Independent of Asset Manager by design.</field>
        </record>
    </data>
</odoo>
